The backchannel AAC decoder is a thread-local Helix instance created once
in aac_openDecoder(). aac_decodeFrm() logs a decode error and returns -1,
leaving the instance wherever the rejected frame left it. A client that
sends a few malformed frames can therefore leave the decoder parsing the
next frame mid-element.

Flush the codec after a hard decode error (anything but an input
underflow, which is normal while a frame is still arriving) so the next
frame starts from a known state. The underlying out-of-bounds read in
libhelix-aac is fixed separately in the libhelix-aac package.

--- a/src/audio/IMPBackchannel.cpp
+++ b/src/audio/IMPBackchannel.cpp
@@ -114,6 +114,10 @@
       LOG_ERROR("Thread-local AAC decode failed: "
                 << ret << " (" << decodeBytesLeft
                 << " bytes left unprocessed)");
+      // A rejected frame can leave the Helix instance in the middle of an
+      // element. Reset it so the next frame is parsed from a known state
+      // instead of whatever the bad bitstream left behind.
+      AACFlushCodec(tl_aacDecoder);
     }
     *outputLengthPtr = 0;
     return (ret == ERR_AAC_INDATA_UNDERFLOW) ? 0 : -1;
